<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG BUILDS ONLY.

  This file overrides src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml for the
  `debug` build type, so a release APK never contains it.

  WHAT IS DIFFERENT, AND WHY IT IS SAFE TO BE
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  One extra trust anchor: the local development certificate authority, bundled
  from certs/rootCA.pem into res/raw by `node scripts/dev-certs.mjs`.

  This is what makes the app work against a development backend over HTTPS
  WITHOUT cleartext. The API's certificate is issued by that CA, and an Android
  app that does not trust the CA refuses the connection outright
  (ERR_CERT_AUTHORITY_INVALID), which is a genuinely baffling symptom, because
  the identical URL works in the browser on the same phone. The browser trusts
  the OS certificate store, which `mkcert -install` populated; the app does not
  read that store. Trusting the CA inside the app is what makes the two agree.

  The alternatives were both worse:

    * cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" works immediately, but ships bearer
      tokens and OTP codes in the clear on any shared network, and it was the
      previous configuration.

    * asking the user to install a CA certificate through Android Settings.
      Android deliberately makes that awkward, and apps that do not opt into
      user-installed anchors silently fail to trust it.

  WHY IT IS NOT A RELEASE CONCERN
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  This file is in src/debug/, so Android compiles it only into a debug build, and
  the CA is a development certificate authority that exists on one machine and is
  trusted by nothing else.

  It is also NOT committed: `*.pem` is in .gitignore, so a clone has no
  res/raw/mkcert_rootca.pem and therefore cannot build the debug APK until
  `node scripts/dev-certs.mjs` has been run. That is the correct order of
  operations, and the reason `npm run certs` is a prerequisite rather than an
  optional extra.

  If a release build ever genuinely needs to trust a private CA (an internal
  deployment with its own authority, say), that belongs in the main config as a
  deliberate, reviewed change. It is not a debug file, and it is certainly not
  re-enabling cleartext.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!-- Development CA. Present only in debug builds. -->
            <certificates src="@raw/mkcert_rootca" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>